hey this is Sarah with registered nurse  Orion comm and today I want to show you  how to put a tourniquet on a patient  whenever you draw blood or start an IV  you'll have to put a tourniquet on and I  just want to give you a quick video on  how to do that with quick-release it  takes a little practice so after you  watch this video I would try to practice  on someone in your family or friend so  you can get the hang of it so here we go  first what you're going to do is you're  going to explain to the patient where  you're going to do and you're going to  take your left hand in your right hand  and you're going to stretch the  tourniquet as far as you can but being  comfortable to the patient to the back  of the arm and you're going to cross it  like a shoestring okay have it cross and  then you're going to take your dominant  hand and you're going to sort of do a  little loop underneath this area and  then just leave it like so okay and then  you do your start your IV draw your  blood and then when you're done do the  quick release and you're just going to  pull it like so and it comes off and now  I'm going to show you how to do it  really fast because that was sort of  slow motion so this is what it looks  like whenever you do it naturally all  done  so doing that it takes a little practice  so like I said just watch this video  over and over and practice and you'll  get it down in no time if you want to  watch some more videos on some nursing  skills be sure to check out my youtube  channel and subscribe to it and also be  sure to visit my website registered  nurse or en comm it's great for anyone  who's going to nursing school or if  you're a nurse and check out the forum  and thank you so much for watching
 

